/** \file p4_psf.dox
 * \brief User's guide for frozen-model P4 PSF responses and filtering.
 * \author Jared R. Males
 */

/** \addtogroup p4_psf_user_guide
 * @{
 *
 * Frozen-model PSF processing calculates the signed response of each fitted detector-frame P4 model to a configured
 * point-source template. It can persist a compact spatially variable response field and apply that field as a
 * normalized filter without changing the ordinary P4 science image.
 *
 * \section p4_psf_response Frozen signed response
 *
 * Setting `p4.psfFile` enables the response calculation for detector-frame P4. Let
 * \f$F_s(p)\f$ denote the centered PSF template sampled at detector coordinate \f$p\f$ for a unit source centered at
 * \f$s\f$, and let \f$S_k\f$ be the exact cubic sampling operator for predictor \f$k\f$. For search coordinate
 * \f$q\f$ and one supported truncated-PCA coefficient vector \f$\beta_m\f$, the local signed response is
 *
 * \f[
 *   G_{q,m}(s) = F_s(q) - \sum_{k=1}^{K} \beta_{q,m,k}\, S_k[F_s].
 * \f]
 *
 * \f$\beta_m\f$ is the truncated minimum-norm pseudoinverse solution already used by an in-sample science
 * regression. It is accumulated while retained eigenmodes are available; no dense pseudoinverse is formed.
 *
 * With target-frame exclusion, the coefficients depend on target frame \f$t\f$. If \f$E_t\f$ is that target's
 * deleted-row set, P4 trains \f$\beta_{q,t,m}\f$ from \f$X_{E_t^c}\f$ and \f$y_{E_t^c}\f$, and calculates
 *
 * \f[
 *   G_{q,t,m}(s) = F_s(q) - \sum_{k=1}^{K} \beta_{q,t,m,k}\, S_k[F_s].
 * \f]
 *
 * The implementation flattens all local source offsets into a direct-response vector \f$f\f$ and predictor-response
 * matrix \f$Q\f$, then evaluates \f$f-Q\beta_{q,t,m}\f$ while each explicit or factor-downdated target fit is live.
 * It therefore does not substitute the shared in-sample coefficients or retain a separate `K`-element coefficient
 * vector for every target. Template calculation and final response storage use float values. The ordinary shared-
 * coefficient and explicit-held-out probe calculations follow P4's M32D64 policy: predictor-response operands,
 * coefficient application, and PCA arithmetic use FP32 around an FP64 eigensolve. The opt-in exact factor-deletion
 * probe path and its whole-search-pixel fallback remain FP64. Invalid and rank-unsupported target/mode fits have
 * explicit zero validity and are never interpreted as a zero PSF.
 *
 * The response is `FROZEN_SIGNED`: it is the fitted linear predictor's signed output with its coefficients held
 * fixed. The stored template scale is preserved; P4 applies no peak or integral normalization and no preprocessing to
 * it. The template must therefore represent the post-preprocessing P4 input stage. This is a local filter template,
 * not a calibrated finite-amplitude throughput measurement, because refitting after fake injection can change the
 * coefficients.
 * Its filtering role is specifically intended to avoid a negative-companion fit followed by a second zero-signal
 * fake-injection reduction merely to obtain a matched-filter template. Full fake injection and refitting remain
 * validation oracles for nonlinear throughput; they are not required inputs to the sparse filtering path.
 *
 * Exact detector-local responses are retained at every owned search coordinate. Sparse shared-model runs retain
 * rank and geometry validity everywhere but calculate coefficient vectors and local responses only at detector
 * search pixels required by the selected final stamps. Final-frame reconstruction maps each sky stamp sample through
 * the exact inverse derotation, composes the same cubic footprints used by science derotation and local sampling, and
 * combines its bounded frame stack with the configured science estimator, weights, minimum-good fraction, and
 * response validity, except that active science `sigmaMean` is mapped to an unclipped mean for the analytic response.
 * Configured weights remain active, so a weighted sigma-clipped science image receives a weighted-mean response.
 * Mean and weighted-mean responses are linear frozen-model products. Median products remain filter-template
 * approximations; they are not finite-amplitude-independent photometric transfer functions. Product provenance
 * records the configured science combination separately from the effective response combination.
 *
 * \section p4_psf_radial Sparse radial approximation
 *
 * Setting either `p4.psfSampleRadii` or `p4.psfRadiiPerRegion` with exactly one angular control replaces the exact
 * response measurement at every search pixel with a sparse polar experiment. Explicit radii use one global radial
 * model. A positive `p4.psfRadiiPerRegion` instead places that many uniformly spaced interior nodes in every P4 search
 * annulus. Candidate detector pixels must belong to the node's annulus, and reconstruction clamps or interpolates
 * only between nodes in the requested pixel's annulus. It never blends response templates across a boundary where
 * the P4 optimization-region geometry changes. `p4.psfSamplesPerRadius` requests a fixed number of uniformly spaced
 * angles at every radius. Alternatively, `p4.psfSampleArcStep` requests
 * \f$max(1,\lceil 2\pi r/\Delta s\rceil)\f$ angles at radius \f$r\f$, so the azimuthal arc between samples is at most
 * \f$\Delta s\f$ pixels. P4 selects the nearest distinct owned search pixel whose actual radius differs from the
 * requested node by no more than \f$\sqrt{1/2}\f$ pixels, the maximum radial displacement introduced by rounding a
 * continuous position to the nearest integer detector pixel. Integer-pixel collisions are removed within that
 * radius, so the realized count can be smaller than the requested count near the origin.
 * `p4.psfSamplingMode=skyExact`, the default, reconstructs every selected sky stamp through all target frames.
 * Before regression, P4 forms a conservative union of the complete cubic derotation footprints for those stamps.
 * This preserves the exact sampled response but can still require every detector-local operator when field rotation
 * and angular sampling cover the full annulus.
 *
 * `p4.psfSamplingMode=detectorLocal` is the explicitly approximate, genuinely sparse alternative. It calculates
 * coefficients and the frozen local operator only at the selected detector pixels. Each operator is sampled as a
 * source-centered response while holding its coefficients fixed across the output stamp, rotated to the common
 * radial orientation, and averaged with the other measurements at that radius. This omits spatial coefficient
 * variation across neighboring target pixels and omits exact propagation of a sampled sky source through all field
 * angles. It initially requires `p4.numberImages=0` and `adi.excludeMethod=none`. Active science sigma clipping is
 * supported through the same unclipped analytic-response rule described above. Ordinary science residuals and
 * rank/geometry validity are still calculated at every owned search pixel.
 *
 * With positive `p4.psfSampleAvoidRadius` and configured `planet.*` metadata, detector-local candidates are rejected
 * if they lie within that distance of a known companion at any target-frame field angle. Selection then shifts in
 * angle to the nearest remaining coordinate in the same radial shell; it never substitutes a pixel at a materially
 * different radius. The response is still measured from the original science fit: no negative
 * companion or zero-signal fake injection is performed. The product records the avoidance radius, rejected candidate
 * count, sampling mode, and realized local-response set. If no uncontaminated detector candidate remains, selection
 * fails rather than silently sampling a known signal.
 *
 * Each measured response stamp is rotated by its actual source angle in the production image-rotation
 * convention so that radial separation points along the positive-column axis. Valid samples in the same radial bin
 * are averaged element by element. To evaluate the model at an arbitrary search pixel, P4 linearly blends the two
 * bracketing canonical radial averages, requires each blended sample to be valid in both endpoints, rotates the
 * result back to the pixel's angle, and expands it into the same per-search-pixel product layout consumed by
 * `hciAnalyze`. Requests outside the applicable global or region-local sampled interval use the nearest endpoint.
 * Sparse radial mode requires an odd response stamp. Exact sky sampling records product schema 3; global
 * detector-local sampling records schema 4; region-aware sampling records schema 5 and `REGION_RADIAL_LINEAR`.
 * Sparse products record the resolved radii, their region ownership, requested counts per radius, sampling mode,
 * realized distinct measurement count, and `HIERARCH P4 LOCAL PSF RESPONSE SEARCH COUNT`. P4 additionally records
 * the radial shell tolerance and the realized count and actual minimum/maximum detector radius at every resolved
 * node.
 *
 * With `p4.numberImages>0`, each row has one temporal predictor: the `mean` or `median` of the central-pixel samples
 * from its exact selected earlier/later frames. One-sided beginning/end fallback is therefore propagated consistently
 * with the science regression. Frozen PSF outputs support the linear `mean` statistic, evaluating the selected source
 * samples against the full prepared template during reconstruction. The nonlinear `median` statistic remains available
 * for science reductions but cannot produce frozen PSF output or filtering products. The gap-held-out PCAT temporal
 * predictor is not yet supported for frozen PSF products and is rejected rather than approximated.
 *
 * Target-frame exclusion and temporal augmentation are deliberately mutually exclusive. With a non-`none`
 * `adi.excludeMethod`, `p4.numberImages` must be zero, `p4.temporalPredictor` must not be `pcat`, and each target response uses the same deleted rows, solver,
 * rank decision, and explicit-fallback policy as its science residual.
 *
 * The implementation requires detector-frame P4 and `adi.postMedSub=false`. A complete final PSF field additionally
 * requires a final combination method; `combine.method=none` does not define a combined response.
 *
 * \section p4_psf_filter Spatially variable filtering
 *
 * With `p4.psfFilter=true`, the final signed response at output coordinate \f$q\f$ is applied directly to the
 * corresponding science neighborhood without constructing a dense space-variant convolution matrix:
 *
 * \f[
 *   \widehat a(q) =
 *   \frac{\sum_{\delta \in V_q} H_q(\delta) I(q+\delta)}
 *        {\sum_{\delta \in V_q} H_q(\delta)^2}.
 * \f]
 *
 * Here \f$V_q\f$ contains response-valid samples whose science coordinates are in bounds and finite. The signed
 * numerator preserves negative subtraction lobes; the denominator normalizes the result to an amplitude relative to
 * the stored template scale. Filtering requires an odd stamp so every response sample maps to an integer science
 * pixel. A result is valid only when the denominator is positive and `p4.psfFilterMinGoodFract` does not exceed
 * \f$|V_q|/W^2\f$. Invalid amplitudes remain NaN rather than becoming zero.
 *
 * \section p4_psf_configuration Configuration
 *
 * | CLI | Config key/value shorthand | PSF-product FITS keyword | Type and limits | Default | Meaning |
 * |---|---|---|---|---|---|
 * | `--p4.psfFile` | `p4.psfFile=/path/template.fits` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF TEMPLATE` | readable, finite two-dimensional FITS image | unset | Centered PSF template at the post-preprocessing P4 input stage. Setting the path enables response calculation without changing the science products. The stored normalization is preserved, including when `p4.numberImages>0`. |
 * | `--p4.psfStampSize` | `p4.psfStampSize=11` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF STAMP SIZE` | positive integer | `0` | Square final response-stamp size. Odd and even sizes use geometric centers. Required with `psfFile`. |
 * | `--p4.psfSampleRadii` | `p4.psfSampleRadii=6,10,14` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF SAMPLE RADII` | strictly increasing nonnegative real pixels inside configured annuli | unset | Enables the sparse radial approximation when paired with exactly one positive angular control. Requires `psfFile` and an odd stamp. |
 * | `--p4.psfRadiiPerRegion` | `p4.psfRadiiPerRegion=2` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF RADII PER REGION` | nonnegative integer, mutually exclusive with `psfSampleRadii` | `0` | Generates uniformly spaced interior radial nodes in every P4 search region. Sampling and interpolation remain inside each region. |
 * | `--p4.psfSamplesPerRadius` | `p4.psfSamplesPerRadius=8` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF SAMPLES PER RADIUS` | positive integer, mutually exclusive with `psfSampleArcStep` | `0` | Fixed number of uniformly spaced requested angles at every sparse sample radius. Nearest-pixel collisions are measured only once per radius. |
 * | `--p4.psfSampleArcStep` | `p4.psfSampleArcStep=3.6` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF SAMPLE ARC STEP` | positive real pixels, mutually exclusive with `psfSamplesPerRadius` | `0` | Maximum azimuthal arc spacing; resolves a radius-dependent angular count no smaller than one. |
 * | `--p4.psfSamplingMode` | `p4.psfSamplingMode=detectorLocal` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF SAMPLING MODE` | `skyExact` or `detectorLocal` | `skyExact` | `skyExact` propagates each sparse sky measurement through all frames. `detectorLocal` calculates only the sampled local operators and applies the documented spatially local filtering approximation. |
 * | `--p4.psfSampleAvoidRadius` | `p4.psfSampleAvoidRadius=5` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF SAMPLE AVOID RADIUS` | nonnegative real detector pixels | `0` | Reject detector-local candidates within this radius of any configured known-planet trajectory. Zero disables avoidance. |
 * | `--p4.outputPSFModels` | `p4.outputPSFModels=true` | — | bool | `false` | Reconstruct, combine, and write one compact final-frame response field per P4 mode. Requires `psfFile` and a final combination. |
 * | `--p4.psfFilter` | `p4.psfFilter=true` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF FILTER` | bool | `false` | Write signed normalized spatially variable filter products. Requires `psfFile`, an odd stamp, and a final combination; it does not require `outputPSFModels=true`. |
 * | `--p4.psfFilterMinGoodFract` | `p4.psfFilterMinGoodFract=0.8` | `HIERARCH P4 PSF FILTER MIN GOOD FRACTION` | real in `[0,1]` | `1` | Minimum fraction of the full stamp with valid PSF samples and finite in-bounds science samples. |
 * | `--p4.psfOutputPrefix` | `p4.psfOutputPrefix=p4PSF_` | — | nonempty path prefix | `p4PSF_` | Compact model and manifest prefix inside the resolved final image's `_outputs` directory. |
 *
 * For example:
 *
 * \code{.ini}
 * [p4]
 * psfFile=/path/template.fits
 * psfStampSize=11
 * psfRadiiPerRegion=2
 * psfSamplesPerRadius=4
 * psfSamplingMode=detectorLocal
 * psfSampleAvoidRadius=5
 * outputPSFModels=true
 * psfFilter=true
 * psfFilterMinGoodFract=0.8
 * \endcode
 *
 * To calculate leave-one-out science and response products together, add:
 *
 * \code{.ini}
 * [adi]
 * excludeMethod=imno
 * minDPx=0
 *
 * [p4]
 * numberImages=0
 * exclusionSolver=factorDowndateExact
 * deletionBackend=rankOneSecular
 * \endcode
 *
 * The template is sampled with zero exterior padding, while the retained local response expands as needed to
 * preserve odd/even sampling phase and arbitrary derotation angles. The P4 memory policy includes the local response,
 * prepared template, one output-mode field, coordinate/index maps, and bounded per-worker frame-stamp scratch.
 * Filtering additionally accounts for four full-frame output cubes. Under target exclusion, local response storage
 * also scales with target count. P4 selects a bounded output-mode batch from `p4.memoryFraction`; a smaller batch
 * repeats the immutable held-out fit for later modes, while a sufficiently large budget evaluates all modes in one
 * response pass. Response fitting and reconstruction independently lower their worker counts when needed and never
 * increase the `OMP_NUM_THREADS` limit. Setting `p4.memoryFraction=0` disables this protection and attempts one batch
 * containing every requested mode.
 *
 * \section p4_psf_products Products and completion contract
 *
 * P4 keeps the ordinary and filtered science products directly in `output.directory`. All other frozen-model PSF
 * products are grouped in a sibling directory derived from the resolved ordinary final-image name. For example,
 * `finim_0044.fits` uses `finim_0044_outputs/`, while an exact name `science.fits` uses `science_outputs/`.
 *
 * With `p4.outputPSFModels=true`, that derived directory receives:
 *
 * - `<prefix>coordinates.fits`, an `S x 4` image with `row,column,region,regionSearch` columns;
 * - `<prefix>model_NNNN.fits`, a `psfStampSize x psfStampSize x S` cube per zero-based mode index;
 * - `<prefix>validity_NNNN.fits`, an exact zero/one source-center validity image per mode; and
 * - `<prefix>manifest.fits`, a one-element completion product.
 *
 * Plane `s` in each model cube belongs to row `s` of the coordinate image. Individual unsupported stamp samples are
 * NaN even when the source center is valid. Exact per-pixel model-only sets use schema version 1; exact per-pixel
 * filtering sets use schema version 2; sparse exact-sky radial sets use schema version 3; sparse detector-local
 * radial sets use schema version 4. Every
 * product records its role, template identity/stage/normalization, `FROZEN_SIGNED` convention,
 * configured science combination, effective analytic-response combination, their respective sigma thresholds,
 * support geometry, storage estimates, source count, local-response search count, and mode identity where applicable.
 * `HIERARCH P4 PSF COEFFICIENT SCOPE` is `SHARED_IN_SAMPLE` for the ordinary model or `TARGET_HELD_OUT` when every
 * frame uses its own deleted-row fit; `HIERARCH P4 PSF MODE BATCH` records the selected maximum held-out batch size.
 * Zero identifies the shared in-sample path, and the final held-out batch may contain fewer modes.
 *
 * For a completed target-held-out set, the manifest also records response-pass downdate clamps, explicit target-row
 * refit invocations, fallback search/batch fits separated by rank-boundary, factor-validation, and deletion-solver
 * cause, and the largest factor-orthogonality defect with its paired tolerance. Counts accumulate over response
 * batches: if the same search fit falls back in two batches, its row refits and fallback fit are counted twice. These
 * totals describe the frozen-response calculation, not the earlier science-image regression. Recoverable response
 * failures are recomputed with the explicit retained-row oracle and reported as warnings; the published model never
 * mixes a partial failed downdate with explicit results.
 *
 * With `p4.psfFilter=true`, four full-image cubes follow the resolved ordinary final-image name and sequence number.
 * The filtered science product remains beside the ordinary final image:
 *
 * - `finim_filtered_NNNN.fits`, containing \f$\sum(HI)/\sum(H^2)\f$ where valid;
 *
 * The three filter diagnostics are placed in `finim_NNNN_outputs/`:
 *
 * - `finim_filter_normalization_NNNN.fits`, containing the retained \f$\sum H^2\f$ denominator;
 * - `finim_filter_support_NNNN.fits`, containing usable samples divided by the full stamp area; and
 * - `finim_filter_validity_NNNN.fits`, containing exact zero/one validity.
 *
 * Filtering is independent of model persistence. `psfFilter=true, outputPSFModels=false` writes the filter products
 * and manifest without coordinate or model cubes. The ordinary final image is never replaced. Each filtered product
 * mirrors its complete FITS header and adds PSF/filter provenance. With an exact final filename, the product role is
 * inserted before the extension, and the diagnostics use the exact final-image stem for their `_outputs` directory.
 *
 * Every file is published by temporary-file rename. The manifest is initially published with
 * `HIERARCH P4 PSF COMPLETE = 0` and replaced with value `1` only after the entire configured set is written.
 * Response diagnostics in the completed manifest are final totals over every output-mode batch. Consumers must
 * require value `1`, use the manifest mode count, and ignore stale extra files from an earlier run.
 *
 * @}
 */
